{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b C1 \u183? a gap filled in an existing stream\par}
{\pard\li720\ri720\sa160\i Existing models show how theories guide learning, how Bayesian agents discriminate among represented causal structures, and how play can reveal previously unavailable actions. This paper models a different transition: an agent's causal language pools objectively different situations; strategic experience endogenously reveals an action-relevant failure of that pooling and thereby creates a localized open question; costly inquiry activates a fallible technology whose output is not measurable in the agent's prior awareness; only after that output is formulated does the agent construct a prior and undertake Bayesian judgment, under an explicit account of how much confidence the generating experience licenses. Because actions depend on theories, play can either create the contrasts that generate questions or suppress them, so the model also delivers the population-level answer: when theory heterogeneity and performance differences persist, and when the market erases them.\par}
{\pard\sa160 Karni and Vier\u248? (W2145868584, W2115460181) already establish that Bayesian conditioning cannot expand a decision maker's universe and give a rule for beliefs after awareness grows, and Gavetti and Levinthal (W2113812196) and Ehrig and Schmidt (W4206921982) already model coarse or incomplete representations that guide search and can be changed; each overlaps a part of the claim, but none has the change of language triggered endogenously by strategic play exposing a pooling failure, a costly and fallible formulation step preceding prior construction, an account of the confidence that experience licenses, or the population-level persistence result. No candidate anticipates the claimed transition as a whole, so it fills a gap between the growing-awareness stream and the theory-based learning stream; Felin and Zenger (W2062762321) give the informal version of experience-triggered theorizing, and for the population-level part the set offers only background on persistent performance differences from other sources (W2160117959, complexity; W2108767715, differing expectations).\par}
{\pard\sa160 Agreement: 3/3\par}
\trowd\trgaph108\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx2340\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx4680\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx7020\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx9360{\pard\intbl\sa0\b Prior work\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0\b Relation\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0\b Basis\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0\b What it establishes\cell}\row
\trowd\trgaph108\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx2340\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx4680\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx7020\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx9360{\pard\intbl\sa0 Karni & Vier\u248? (2013). \u8220?Reverse Bayesianism\u8221?: A Choice-Based Theory of Growing Awareness \u183? doi ({\f1 https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.103.7.2790})\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 overlaps\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 introduction\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 An axiomatic choice-based model of growing awareness in which discovering new acts, consequences or act-consequence links expands the state space (something Bayesian conditioning cannot do), with beliefs on the expanded space obtained by proportional 'reverse Bayesian' reallocation of probability mass.\cell}\row
\trowd\trgaph108\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx2340\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx4680\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx7020\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx9360{\pard\intbl\sa0 Gavetti & Levinthal (2000). Looking Forward and Looking Backward: Cognitive and Experiential Search \u183? doi ({\f1 https://doi.org/10.2307/2666981})\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 overlaps\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 introduction\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 A simulation model in which actors search using a low-dimensional cognitive representation of a higher-dimensional fitness landscape; crude representations guide and constrain experiential search, and changing the representation is itself a mode of adaptation, at the cost of tacit knowledge built under the prior cognition.\cell}\row
\trowd\trgaph108\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx2340\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx4680\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx7020\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx9360{\pard\intbl\sa0 Ehrig & Schmidt (2022). Theory\u8208?based learning and experimentation: How strategists can systematically generate knowledge at the edge between the known and the unknown \u183? doi ({\f1 https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.3381})\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 overlaps\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 introduction\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 A framework, with what it calls the first formal model of learning from arguments in strategy, in which strategists' theories are incomplete and possibly wrong, guide experimentation, and can be revised by testing necessary conditions and identifying overlooked critical contingencies.\cell}\row
\trowd\trgaph108\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx2340\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx4680\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx7020\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx9360{\pard\intbl\sa0 Karni & Vier\u248? (2017). Awareness of unawareness: A theory of decision making in the face of ignorance \u183? doi ({\f1 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jet.2016.12.011})\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 background\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 introduction\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 Extends reverse Bayesianism to a decision maker aware of her own unawareness, with a state space partitioned into fully describable and partially or non-describable states, a 'utility of the unknown', and belief evolution about residual ignorance as actions reveal unforeseen consequences.\cell}\row
\trowd\trgaph108\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx2340\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx4680\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx7020\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx9360{\pard\intbl\sa0 Barney (1986). Strategic Factor Markets: Expectations, Luck, and Business Strategy \u183? doi ({\f1 https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.32.10.1231})\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 background\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 abstract\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 Strategic factor markets yield above-normal performance only when firms hold different expectations about a resource's future value, so persistent performance differences trace to heterogeneous expectations rather than to product-market position.\cell}\row{\pard\sa160\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b Coverage\par}
{\pard\sa160 Venues searched: utd24, ft50, cabs4star, cabs4 (150 journals, 150 resolved in OpenAlex). 4 queries from the contribution claim and the title returned 25 hits; 20 works were added as the most cited among them (15 such works lay outside the venues and were dropped). Candidates shown to the model: 45, of which 5 have no abstract and were seen by title, venue and year only. 19 were also read from their introduction (10 from the journal's licensed copies, 9 from open-access copies); the rest have no copy the journal holds or could fetch. Not covered: work outside the venues searched, books, work without an abstract, beyond its title, non-English work, grey literature.\par}
